+\begin{lemma}[Innovation split]
+\label{lem:innovation_split}
+Under \eqref{eq:OU_process}--\eqref{eq:affine_b}, every square-integrable
+$\mathbb R^d$-valued function $u$ of $\theta_{t-\tau}$ satisfies
+\begin{equation}
+    \|X_t-u\|_Q^2
+    =\|e^{-\rho}X_{t-\tau}-u\|_Q^2
+      +(1-e^{-2\rho})\,2R_\infty.
+    \label{eq:innovation_split}
+\end{equation}
+\end{lemma}
+\begin{proof}
+The error is the sum of $e^{-\rho}X_{t-\tau}-u$ and
+$K\varepsilon_{t,\tau}$. The first term is a function of the old snapshot,
+so the conditional mean in \eqref{eq:state_innovation_decomposition} makes
+their $Q$-inner product zero. Applying the same orthogonality with $u=0$
+and using stationarity gives
+\[
+    \|K\varepsilon_{t,\tau}\|_Q^2
+    =\|X_t\|_Q^2-e^{-2\rho}\|X_{t-\tau}\|_Q^2
+    =(1-e^{-2\rho})\,2R_\infty.
+\]
+Adding the two orthogonal error costs proves \eqref{eq:innovation_split}.
+\end{proof}
+
+The first term is the error in reproducing the predictable old decision;
